## 1. Business write + event in one transaction
   concept: orders row and outbox row commit together; Debezium publishes the outbox row, the app never talks to Kafka
   outbox <- OrderPlaced id=8669e91d-8ad4-440e-ba13-8b90265f1433

## 2. Every state change emits an intent-level event
   concept: the event says OrderPaid, not 'status column changed'
   outbox <- OrderPaid id=68b08a4f-fd47-4ebd-b1ba-34d2dc1dde8c

## 3. Rollback drops the event too
   concept: business write fails -> transaction rolls back -> outbox row never existed -> no event
   outbox <- OrderPlaced id=873b3d35-290e-4198-ac68-67fc47f2c4dc
   rejected: payment provider down, order 2 aborted

## 4. Outbox can be cleaned immediately
   concept: insert + delete in one tx: table stays small, Debezium reads the insert from the WAL, EventRouter ignores the delete
   outbox <- OrderShipped id=7e4f3e83-6d16-4069-9017-8acfb572218f
   all outbox rows for this order deleted, OrderShipped in the transaction that inserted it (the WAL still has every insert)

## 5. Contrast: naive dual write (simulated crash)
   concept: commit DB then publish separately: a crash between the two loses the event forever
   order 3 committed to Postgres
   process crashes before producer.send(OrderPlaced) -> nothing will ever publish it

